{"id": 71, "timestamp": "2025-12-19T18:57:41.354034", "source": "agent", "message": "", "action": "edit", "tool_call_metadata": {"function_name": "str_replace_editor", "tool_call_id": "toolu_32", "model_response": {"id": "msg_vrtx_019Zu1jEjtFaAPK1ba5Mu1kg", "created": 1766170661, "model": "claude-sonnet-4", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "Let me create a summary document explaining the fix:", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"index": 1, "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"create\", \"path\": \"/app/fix_summary.md\", \"file_text\": \"\\n# Fix for Nested CompoundModel Separability Issue\\n\\n## Problem Description\\n\\nThe `separability_matrix` function in astropy's modeling module was not computing separability correctly for nested CompoundModels. Specifically, when using models like:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom astropy.modeling import models as m\\nfrom astropy.modeling.separable import separability_matrix\\n\\ncm = m.Linear1D(10) & m.Linear1D(5)\\nnested_model = m.Pix2Sky_TAN() & cm\\n```\\n\\nThe separability matrix for `nested_model` would incorrectly show:\\n```\\narray([[ True,  True, False, False],\\n       [ True,  True, False, False],\\n       [False, False,  True,  True],  # <- Wrong: should be [True, False]\\n       [False, False,  True,  True]]) # <- Wrong: should be [False, True]\\n```\\n\\nInstead of the expected:\\n```\\narray([[ True,  True, False, False],\\n       [ True,  True, False, False],\\n       [False, False,  True, False],  # <- Correct\\n       [False, False, False,  True]]) # <- Correct\\n```\\n\\n## Root Cause\\n\\nThe bug was in the `_cstack` function in `astropy/modeling/separable.py` at line 245 (original code):\\n\\n```python\\n# BUGGY CODE (line 245)\\ncright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = 1\\n```\\n\\nThis line was setting all values in the right portion of the matrix to `1`, which destroyed the separability information that had been previously computed for nested compound models.\\n\\nWhen processing `Pix2Sky_TAN() & cm`, the function would:\\n1. Correctly compute the separability matrix for `cm` (which is `[[1, 0], [0, 1]]`)\\n2. But then overwrite this with all `1`s in line 245, resulting in `[[1, 1], [1, 1]]`\\n\\n## Solution\\n\\nChanged line 245 from:\\n```python\\ncright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = 1\\n```\\n\\nTo:\\n```python\\ncright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = right\\n```\\n\\nThis preserves the existing separability matrix structure instead of overwriting it with all `1`s.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\nThe fix has been verified with:\\n\\n1. **Standalone test**: Created a simulation that shows the buggy behavior and confirms the fix resolves the issue.\\n\\n2. **Comprehensive test**: Added a regression test `test_nested_compound_model_separability()` to `test_separable.py` that specifically tests this scenario.\\n\\n3. **Backward compatibility**: The fix only affects the case where `right` is already a computed matrix (i.e., from a nested compound model). All existing functionality remains unchanged.\\n\\n## Test Results\\n\\nWith the fix applied:\\n- Simple compound models: `Linear1D & Linear1D` \\u2192 Correct diagonal matrix \\u2713\\n- Complex models: `Pix2Sky_TAN & Linear1D & Linear1D` \\u2192 Correct matrix \\u2713  \\n- Nested models: `Pix2Sky_TAN & (Linear1D & Linear1D)` \\u2192 Now equals complex model \\u2713\\n\\nThe nested model now correctly shows that the outputs from the `Linear1D` models are independent (separable), which matches the behavior of the non-nested equivalent.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nThis fix ensures that:\\n1. Nested compound models have correct separability analysis\\n2. Model composition and optimization can work correctly with nested models\\n3. The separability information is preserved through all levels of model nesting\\n4. No existing functionality is affected\\n\\n\"}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}, "id": "toolu_32", "type": "function"}], "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 940, "prompt_tokens": 29760, "total_tokens": 30700, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}, "extra_tokens_details": {"CacheCreationInputTokens": 29758}}, "service_tier": null}, "total_calls_in_response": 1}, "llm_metrics": {"accumulated_cost": 0.0, "max_budget_per_task": null, "accumulated_token_usage": {"model": "default", "prompt_tokens": 554199, "completion_tokens": 11501, "cache_read_tokens": 203317, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "context_window": 0, "per_turn_token": 30700, "response_id": ""}, "costs": [], "response_latencies": [], "token_usages": []}, "args": {"path": "/app/fix_summary.md", "command": "create", "file_text": "\n# Fix for Nested CompoundModel Separability Issue\n\n## Problem Description\n\nThe `separability_matrix` function in astropy's modeling module was not computing separability correctly for nested CompoundModels. Specifically, when using models like:\n\n```python\nfrom astropy.modeling import models as m\nfrom astropy.modeling.separable import separability_matrix\n\ncm = m.Linear1D(10) & m.Linear1D(5)\nnested_model = m.Pix2Sky_TAN() & cm\n```\n\nThe separability matrix for `nested_model` would incorrectly show:\n```\narray([[ True,  True, False, False],\n       [ True,  True, False, False],\n       [False, False,  True,  True],  # <- Wrong: should be [True, False]\n       [False, False,  True,  True]]) # <- Wrong: should be [False, True]\n```\n\nInstead of the expected:\n```\narray([[ True,  True, False, False],\n       [ True,  True, False, False],\n       [False, False,  True, False],  # <- Correct\n       [False, False, False,  True]]) # <- Correct\n```\n\n## Root Cause\n\nThe bug was in the `_cstack` function in `astropy/modeling/separable.py` at line 245 (original code):\n\n```python\n# BUGGY CODE (line 245)\ncright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = 1\n```\n\nThis line was setting all values in the right portion of the matrix to `1`, which destroyed the separability information that had been previously computed for nested compound models.\n\nWhen processing `Pix2Sky_TAN() & cm`, the function would:\n1. Correctly compute the separability matrix for `cm` (which is `[[1, 0], [0, 1]]`)\n2. But then overwrite this with all `1`s in line 245, resulting in `[[1, 1], [1, 1]]`\n\n## Solution\n\nChanged line 245 from:\n```python\ncright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = 1\n```\n\nTo:\n```python\ncright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = right\n```\n\nThis preserves the existing separability matrix structure instead of overwriting it with all `1`s.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe fix has been verified with:\n\n1. **Standalone test**: Created a simulation that shows the buggy behavior and confirms the fix resolves the issue.\n\n2. **Comprehensive test**: Added a regression test `test_nested_compound_model_separability()` to `test_separable.py` that specifically tests this scenario.\n\n3. **Backward compatibility**: The fix only affects the case where `right` is already a computed matrix (i.e., from a nested compound model). All existing functionality remains unchanged.\n\n## Test Results\n\nWith the fix applied:\n- Simple compound models: `Linear1D & Linear1D` → Correct diagonal matrix ✓\n- Complex models: `Pix2Sky_TAN & Linear1D & Linear1D` → Correct matrix ✓  \n- Nested models: `Pix2Sky_TAN & (Linear1D & Linear1D)` → Now equals complex model ✓\n\nThe nested model now correctly shows that the outputs from the `Linear1D` models are independent (separable), which matches the behavior of the non-nested equivalent.\n\n## Impact\n\nThis fix ensures that:\n1. Nested compound models have correct separability analysis\n2. Model composition and optimization can work correctly with nested models\n3. The separability information is preserved through all levels of model nesting\n4. No existing functionality is affected\n\n", "old_str": null, "new_str": null, "insert_line": null, "content": "", "start": 1, "end": -1, "thought": "Let me create a summary document explaining the fix:", "impl_source": "oh_aci"}}
{"id": 72, "timestamp": "2025-12-19T18:57:41.363642", "source": "agent", "message": "I edited the file /app/fix_summary.md.", "cause": 71, "observation": "edit", "tool_call_metadata": {"function_name": "str_replace_editor", "tool_call_id": "toolu_32", "model_response": {"id": "msg_vrtx_019Zu1jEjtFaAPK1ba5Mu1kg", "created": 1766170661, "model": "claude-sonnet-4", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "Let me create a summary document explaining the fix:", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"index": 1, "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"create\", \"path\": \"/app/fix_summary.md\", \"file_text\": \"\\n# Fix for Nested CompoundModel Separability Issue\\n\\n## Problem Description\\n\\nThe `separability_matrix` function in astropy's modeling module was not computing separability correctly for nested CompoundModels. Specifically, when using models like:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom astropy.modeling import models as m\\nfrom astropy.modeling.separable import separability_matrix\\n\\ncm = m.Linear1D(10) & m.Linear1D(5)\\nnested_model = m.Pix2Sky_TAN() & cm\\n```\\n\\nThe separability matrix for `nested_model` would incorrectly show:\\n```\\narray([[ True,  True, False, False],\\n       [ True,  True, False, False],\\n       [False, False,  True,  True],  # <- Wrong: should be [True, False]\\n       [False, False,  True,  True]]) # <- Wrong: should be [False, True]\\n```\\n\\nInstead of the expected:\\n```\\narray([[ True,  True, False, False],\\n       [ True,  True, False, False],\\n       [False, False,  True, False],  # <- Correct\\n       [False, False, False,  True]]) # <- Correct\\n```\\n\\n## Root Cause\\n\\nThe bug was in the `_cstack` function in `astropy/modeling/separable.py` at line 245 (original code):\\n\\n```python\\n# BUGGY CODE (line 245)\\ncright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = 1\\n```\\n\\nThis line was setting all values in the right portion of the matrix to `1`, which destroyed the separability information that had been previously computed for nested compound models.\\n\\nWhen processing `Pix2Sky_TAN() & cm`, the function would:\\n1. Correctly compute the separability matrix for `cm` (which is `[[1, 0], [0, 1]]`)\\n2. But then overwrite this with all `1`s in line 245, resulting in `[[1, 1], [1, 1]]`\\n\\n## Solution\\n\\nChanged line 245 from:\\n```python\\ncright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = 1\\n```\\n\\nTo:\\n```python\\ncright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = right\\n```\\n\\nThis preserves the existing separability matrix structure instead of overwriting it with all `1`s.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\nThe fix has been verified with:\\n\\n1. **Standalone test**: Created a simulation that shows the buggy behavior and confirms the fix resolves the issue.\\n\\n2. **Comprehensive test**: Added a regression test `test_nested_compound_model_separability()` to `test_separable.py` that specifically tests this scenario.\\n\\n3. **Backward compatibility**: The fix only affects the case where `right` is already a computed matrix (i.e., from a nested compound model). All existing functionality remains unchanged.\\n\\n## Test Results\\n\\nWith the fix applied:\\n- Simple compound models: `Linear1D & Linear1D` \\u2192 Correct diagonal matrix \\u2713\\n- Complex models: `Pix2Sky_TAN & Linear1D & Linear1D` \\u2192 Correct matrix \\u2713  \\n- Nested models: `Pix2Sky_TAN & (Linear1D & Linear1D)` \\u2192 Now equals complex model \\u2713\\n\\nThe nested model now correctly shows that the outputs from the `Linear1D` models are independent (separable), which matches the behavior of the non-nested equivalent.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nThis fix ensures that:\\n1. Nested compound models have correct separability analysis\\n2. Model composition and optimization can work correctly with nested models\\n3. The separability information is preserved through all levels of model nesting\\n4. No existing functionality is affected\\n\\n\"}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}, "id": "toolu_32", "type": "function"}], "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 940, "prompt_tokens": 29760, "total_tokens": 30700, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}, "extra_tokens_details": {"CacheCreationInputTokens": 29758}}, "service_tier": null}, "total_calls_in_response": 1}, "content": "File created successfully at: /app/fix_summary.md", "extras": {"path": "/app/fix_summary.md", "prev_exist": false, "old_content": null, "new_content": null, "impl_source": "oh_aci", "diff": "--- /app/fix_summary.md\n+++ /app/fix_summary.md\n@@ -1,0 +2,87 @@\n+# Fix for Nested CompoundModel Separability Issue\n+\n+## Problem Description\n+\n+The `separability_matrix` function in astropy's modeling module was not computing separability correctly for nested CompoundModels. Specifically, when using models like:\n+\n+```python\n+from astropy.modeling import models as m\n+from astropy.modeling.separable import separability_matrix\n+\n+cm = m.Linear1D(10) & m.Linear1D(5)\n+nested_model = m.Pix2Sky_TAN() & cm\n+```\n+\n+The separability matrix for `nested_model` would incorrectly show:\n+```\n+array([[ True,  True, False, False],\n+       [ True,  True, False, False],\n+       [False, False,  True,  True],  # <- Wrong: should be [True, False]\n+       [False, False,  True,  True]]) # <- Wrong: should be [False, True]\n+```\n+\n+Instead of the expected:\n+```\n+array([[ True,  True, False, False],\n+       [ True,  True, False, False],\n+       [False, False,  True, False],  # <- Correct\n+       [False, False, False,  True]]) # <- Correct\n+```\n+\n+## Root Cause\n+\n+The bug was in the `_cstack` function in `astropy/modeling/separable.py` at line 245 (original code):\n+\n+```python\n+# BUGGY CODE (line 245)\n+cright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = 1\n+```\n+\n+This line was setting all values in the right portion of the matrix to `1`, which destroyed the separability information that had been previously computed for nested compound models.\n+\n+When processing `Pix2Sky_TAN() & cm`, the function would:\n+1. Correctly compute the separability matrix for `cm` (which is `[[1, 0], [0, 1]]`)\n+2. But then overwrite this with all `1`s in line 245, resulting in `[[1, 1], [1, 1]]`\n+\n+## Solution\n+\n+Changed line 245 from:\n+```python\n+cright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = 1\n+```\n+\n+To:\n+```python\n+cright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = right\n+```\n+\n+This preserves the existing separability matrix structure instead of overwriting it with all `1`s.\n+\n+## Verification\n+\n+The fix has been verified with:\n+\n+1. **Standalone test**: Created a simulation that shows the buggy behavior and confirms the fix resolves the issue.\n+\n+2. **Comprehensive test**: Added a regression test `test_nested_compound_model_separability()` to `test_separable.py` that specifically tests this scenario.\n+\n+3. **Backward compatibility**: The fix only affects the case where `right` is already a computed matrix (i.e., from a nested compound model). All existing functionality remains unchanged.\n+\n+## Test Results\n+\n+With the fix applied:\n+- Simple compound models: `Linear1D & Linear1D` → Correct diagonal matrix ✓\n+- Complex models: `Pix2Sky_TAN & Linear1D & Linear1D` → Correct matrix ✓\n+- Nested models: `Pix2Sky_TAN & (Linear1D & Linear1D)` → Now equals complex model ✓\n+\n+The nested model now correctly shows that the outputs from the `Linear1D` models are independent (separable), which matches the behavior of the non-nested equivalent.\n+\n+## Impact\n+\n+This fix ensures that:\n+1. Nested compound models have correct separability analysis\n+2. Model composition and optimization can work correctly with nested models\n+3. The separability information is preserved through all levels of model nesting\n+4. No existing functionality is affected\n+\n+", "_